#!/bin/sh
# Pre-commit: run the regression suites whenever a source they cover, or any suite
# itself, is staged - so a commit can't silently regress the ASV's obstacle avoidance,
# its Rule 9 handedness, its survey turn geometry, its safety chain or its readouts.
#
# THE SUITE LIST IS DERIVED FROM tests/, NEVER WRITTEN DOWN HERE. This file used to
# carry the names in three places - a header comment, the staged-path filter and the
# run block - and ALL THREE drifted:
#   * the path filter named suites individually and had never listed five of them
#     (wreck_clearance, water_trust, ais_range, live_speed, completion_modes), so
#     editing one of those ALONE ran nothing. Replaced with `tests/`.
#   * the header comment documented seventeen suites while the hook ran twenty.
#   * the run block had to be edited by hand for every new suite, so a suite written
#     and not registered here would never run in anger.
# All three are gone: the loop below globs tests/ and a NEW SUITE IS RUN THE DAY IT IS
# WRITTEN. This is the same "a hand-maintained list beside a directory that already
# answers the question will drift" trap as the pop-out panel copies and the suite
# counts in CLAUDE.md. Read each suite's own docstring for what it guards.
#
# What is NOT derived is the failure ADVICE: each suite gets a line naming the code to
# look at, because "panel_drag failed" is worth much less than "a pop-out grew its own
# private drag again". Those live in advice_for() and are looked up by basename; a suite
# with no entry still RUNS and reports, it just gets the generic message. Missing advice
# is a cosmetic gap - a missing RUN is a hole, which is why only the advice is manual.
#
# Ported from the sibling console 2026-07-31. Everything browser-side lives in ONE file
# here (static/asv.html - there is no routing.js split).
#
# Activated by  git config core.hooksPath .githooks  (run once per clone; see
# README / the hook's own note). Bypass in a pinch with:  git commit --no-verify

# SKIP ONLY WHAT NO SUITE READS (review #17, 2026-09-15). This used to be a list of the paths that DID count,
# and it drifted exactly the way the suite list once had: currents.py, vessels/*.json and ports.default.json
# were never on it, so a commit touching only the vendored current model, a vessel's own file or the default
# ports ran nothing at all. It is inverted now - the suites run unless EVERY staged path is one no suite reads:
# Markdown (README, CLAUDE.md, the other notes) and git's own metadata. A source file added tomorrow is covered
# the day it is added. Nothing staged at all (an amend that only rewords) runs them too: an amend that ran zero
# suites and reported success is how a broken commit got through before.
changed=$(git diff --cached --name-only)
if [ -n "$changed" ] && ! printf '%s\n' "$changed" | grep -qvE '(^|/)[^/]*\.md$|^\.gitignore$|^\.gitattributes$'; then
  exit 0
fi

# Per-suite advice. A suite absent from this list still runs - it just gets the default.
advice_for() {
  case "$1" in
    buoy_lane.js)      echo "  The route search changed the Rule 9 handedness (centreline no longer to port). Fix channelLaneRoute in static/asv.html." ;;
    wreck_clearance.js) echo "  A charted wreck / obstruction no longer keeps a route off it, or the vessel's nogo buffer floor leaked (hazExtent / blocked / rasterKeepouts)." ;;
    water_trust.js)    echo "  A tide reading from far away would no longer be flagged as such (waterTrust / WATER_FAR_KM / WATER_REMOTE_KM in static/js/chart.js) - or, 16-21 (review #13), a stale one would be applied: waterTrust's stale is age_s > WATER_STALE_S for a numeric age only, never for a manual override, and effectiveWaterOffset returns 0 when stale." ;;
    reading_age.js)    echo "  A reading on the card no longer says its age (review #13): updateWaterUI shows ' · N min' and marks a stale level ' ⚠ (datum)' with notApplied, the survey note gives its reasons, updateEnvUI shows envAge unless simOff, updateCurrentUI shows the age past CURRENT_AGE_SHOW_S, and monitorErrTxt names a failed update in each tooltip." ;;
    reading_age.py)    echo "  A monitor can freeze again (review #13): WaterLevel / EnvMonitor / CurrentsMonitor must run their loop body through _monitor_pass (record _error, print on change, keep the last reading, clear on success); snapshots publish age_s and monitor_error; _coops_get catches http.client.HTTPException; CurrentsMonitor samples every SAMPLE_S and keeps _ensure_cycle's reason when there is no cycle." ;;
    speed_recalc.js)   echo "  Changing the plan speed no longer recalculates, or a committed plan is not re-checked against the turn radius the new speed implies (recalcForSpeed)." ;;
    ui_tooltips.js)    echo "  The hover-tip layer broke: tipPos lost its flip/clamp (the pointer can occlude the tip again), the stash stopped suppressing the native tip (two tips at once), the restore stopped guarding for mid-hover title writers, or moving within a control kills its tip (the [title] delegation block in static/asv.html - CRLF file)." ;;
    ui_split.js)       echo "  A window-split registration selector no longer resolves, or the vessel-status card has been mirrored back into the controls window (UI_BRIDGED / UI_CARD_TITLES)." ;;
    stored_settings.js) echo "  A stored setting no longer survives blocked or full storage, a toggle saved by an older build as \"1\"/\"0\" no longer reads, or a bare localStorage call crept back in (lsGet / lsSet / lsDel / lsBool)." ;;
    trail_persist.js)  echo "  The trail stops being saved while the boat moves, or is kept short again (review #21): saveTrack must be a THROTTLE (arm one write TRACK_SAVE_MS after the first new point; never restart the wait), flushTrack must run on pagehide and on visibilitychange to hidden, the append must use distTo >= TRACK_STEP_M, MAX_TRACK must cover the longest boot in the logs (68 km), writeTrack must store microdegree deltas with the boot id and keep the NEWEST part on a quota error (and try once when storage is off), loadTrack must still read the older shapes without throwing, and trailScreenPath must cache each point's zoom-0 world position and skip sub-pixel and off-view vertices against the last one DRAWN (static/asv.html)." ;;
    spawn_trail.js)    echo "  THE TRAIL OUTLIVED THE BOOT IT BELONGS TO. checkBoot must compare the boot id on EVERY frame, not once per page load - against the STORED id on the first frame and the LIVE id after that. It is the only clear that can land on the frame REPORTING the new boot, which is the only clear that also wipes a stale fix pushed in between: the old link keeps ticking at 4 Hz and its next frame lands ~160 ms AFTER the spawn command, so a pre-emptive clearTrack() leaves exactly two points and the chart strokes a 5 km line between the placement the operator abandoned and the one they chose. Watch for: \`if(bootChecked) return\` coming back; the comparison reverting to savedBootId on every frame; a clear on every frame instead of on a CHANGE (that silently deletes the trail as it is drawn - checks 5-7 are the acceptance case); doSpawn clearing ABOVE its ok gate again, which costs the operator the trail and the drawn route on a REFUSED spawn; a bare \`track = []\` returning to resetForNewArea, which leaves the localStorage mirror and the armed save alive to write it back. This suite is worthless without home_spawn.py checks 6b and 7 - an accepted spawn mints a new boot_id, a refused one does not (checkBoot / clearTrack / doSpawn / resetForNewArea in static/asv.html)." ;;
    survey_transit_roles.js) echo "  A SEGMENT IS BEING FLOWN AT THE WRONG ROLE'S SPEED. The rule, from the plan and not from where the boat happens to be: a gap between consecutive lines is a REVERSAL within 4x the plan's own median end-to-start gap, and a TRANSIT beyond it. Watch for: isReversalGap dropped from accumLineTime's turn test, which flies the whole hop to the next coverage region at the TURN speed (measured: 618 m, 304 s at 4.0 kn where the transit speed does it in 84.9 s); the sticky curTurn coming back - a turn must CLOSE when the boat leaves without reaching a line, or a reversal the boat never completes stays a turn for ever; reversalScaleM measuring a-to-a like turnZoneM does, which on a boustrophedon is the LINE LENGTH and not the spacing; the 4x collapsing to 1x, which turns a struck-line gap into a transit; committedRoleLengths billing the gaps as survey again, or returning ZEROS instead of null when it cannot walk the chain (a zero reads as 'no survey' on the card); and the Lines card's two transit rows going back to the survey speed - they are the two legs the operator asked to be transits and the table around them is headed with a different speed (accumLineTime / isReversalGap / committedRoleLengths / renderLineTable in static/asv.html)." ;;
    min_depth_floor.js) echo "  THE OPERATOR'S MIN DEPTH IS A ROUTING FLOOR FOR EVERY BEHAVIOR, not a survey setting - and the floor in force is the DEEPER of it and the hull's own navigability limit, so a Min depth set below what the hull needs can never narrow its clearance (the same rule bufferFloor keeps for the buffer). Watch for: nogoDR dropping the max() and reading only V.NOGO_MIN_DEPTH_M, which is the reported gap coming back; MAX depth being 'finished' by wiring it through, which makes deep water a keep-out and a Go-To across a deep channel unroutable - deep water is not a hazard and the survey Max depth is a COVERAGE window; setMinDepth losing its rebuildNogo, its mirroring or its write to V.OPER_MIN_DEPTH_M, any of which leaves the control cosmetic; either of the two controls being dropped from the always-visible command bar, which is the whole reported fault (with no survey drawn the SURV card is closed, so neither speed nor depth could be set at all); the bar's transit select growing its own writer instead of sharing setRoleSpeed; and min_depth_m falling out of ANY of the four whitelists it has to survive - the client's loadMission rebuild and the server's load_mission, save_mission and empty-file default. Miss save_mission and the field is dropped on every write, which is exactly how \`speeds\` was lost. AND THE TIDE: applyWaterOffset must stay the ONE writer of sea.waterOffset, run before clearanceGuard, and rebuild the model once the level is TIDE_REBUILD_M from nogo.builtOffset - the level it was BUILT at, never the last reading, or 5 cm station steps never add up and a model built at high water keeps a drying flat open (applyWaterOffset / rebuildNogo in static/asv.html, nogoDR in static/js/chart.js, setMinDepth / setRoleSpeed / nogoReadout in static/asv.html, load_mission + save_mission in asv_console.py)." ;;
    ais_table.js)      echo "  The traffic list is being REBUILT rather than patched - that resets the card's scroll position mid-read and drops a text selection every poll (renderAisTable / setCellText)." ;;
    measure_tool.js)   echo "  The chart ruler or its right-click menu broke: the label stopped flowing along the leg (rotated past 90 deg, so it reads upside-down) or stopped going through fmtDist (so it no longer follows the DIST pill), the click-move-click gesture is reading a PAN-drag as a measurement (the measure branch must stay BELOW the 'moved > 5' guard in the window mouseup), a right-click is commanding the chart again (mousedown's 'e.button !== 0' guard), or a menu row re-derived a vessel gate instead of reading its owning button's .disabled - cmGate is meant to be the reason there is only ONE copy of 'armed and not E-STOPped' (measLabel / drawMeasureLeg / openChartMenu in static/asv.html)." ;;
    panel_drag.js)     echo "  A floating pop-out grew its own private drag again beside the shared mechanism, lost the key that remembers where it was put, or stopped releasing its bottom/right anchor (makeDraggablePanel / showPanel / clampPanelPos)." ;;
    survey_card.js)    echo "  The survey card blanks on a committed plan again, or its derived figures disagree with mission.lines (committedPatternInfo in static/asv.html)." ;;
    pattern_move_grip.js) echo "  The survey pattern's whole-move grip is unreachable again - drawn where the boat marker covers it, or losing its hit-test tie-break (drawPatMoveGrip / patHandleAt)." ;;
    nogo_readout.js)   echo "  The Nogo row no longer names its state - stuck reading after a successful extract, or 'no chart' conflated with 'clear water' (nogoReadout / updateNogoUI)." ;;
    target_cpa.js)     echo "  CPA / TCPA or the AIS hull footprint has moved. CPA is the closest two vessels will come on present course and speed and TCPA is when; both are PURE functions of the current kinematics, which is what makes recalculate-after-a-manoeuvre free - there is no cached value to go stale and no manoeuvre detector to miss one. Watch for: a cached CPA appearing (check 6); the tcpa SIGN being clamped, which reports a contact already drawing away as one still closing - the one wrong answer that reads exactly like a working alarm (check 2); the holding-station guard going, so a near-zero relative speed divides into an enormous time (check 4); and the hull box being centred rather than anchored on the GNSS ANTENNA, which on a 295 m ship with the bridge aft puts the stem 77 m from where it is (check 7). ⚠ Checks 10 and 11 reach into the page because the maths was right and the READOUT was not: the boat s own track is on S.status.sog_kn / cog_deg and NOT on asv, and fmtDist takes METRES - dividing by 1000 rendered every sub-kilometre CPA as 0 m, a 434 m pass shown as a collision (static/js/targets.js, aisCpa / cpaText in static/asv.html)." ;;
    ais_particulars.py) echo "  THE VESSEL PARTICULARS AIS ALREADY BROADCASTS have stopped arriving. Message 5 carries the hull dimensions, destination, draught, IMO and call sign; 24 part B and 19 carry type and dimensions. Both decoders used to read name and type and discard the rest, and the aisstream subscription had been asking for ShipStaticData all along - the data was arriving, being parsed and thrown away. Watch for: dimensions read at the wrong ITU offsets (each message has its OWN - 240/249/258/264 on msg 5, 271/280/289/295 on msg 19, 132/141/150/156 on 24B); all-zero dimensions being taken as a real hull rather than as NOT AVAILABLE; AIS text padding surviving as an empty destination that reads as nowhere; a position report clearing the static fields it should merge under; and above all the STATIC_KEYS loop leaving Registry.snapshot(), which is a WHITELIST - a particular can decode, merge and be held correctly and still never reach the console because the serialiser never mentioned it. Tonnage is NOT in any AIS message and its absence is not a bug (decode_aivdm_payload / _static_from_aisstream / Registry in ais_service.py)." ;;
    track_edge.js)     echo "  A SAFETY SUITE, and the one that keeps the boat MOVING. The look-ahead follows the PLAN: it walks the route from the boat's actual position and heading, at the HULL'S OWN turn rate, with the drift added at every step. The straight-line projection it replaced alarmed 40-60 m before the buffer edge that every planner in this console clips and routes TO - so a correctly punched survey line was stopped with 33 m of its own certified water still to run, in dead calm, and a routed Go-To was held 39 m short of its own turn. A false hold is not a harmless pause: it takes the way off a hull the tide is already setting, and it destroys the run (a hold replaces the vessel's plan with one waypoint - there is no resume). Watch for: the projection reverting to a straight line, or losing the drift, or swinging faster than the hull can (a check that reports safe because it modelled a better boat); the route-aware form being used when heading or way is MISSING, which quietly trusts the plan; the projection continuing past the last waypoint (what happens after the plan is the HOLD POINT's question and hold.js certifies it); the deviation being verified at the trigger buffer rather than at buf+margin, which is the hysteresis that makes ONE amendment hold instead of three in twelve seconds; the deviation being applied to the leg being flown rather than the leg that FOULS (on a routed detour the trouble is usually a corner two or three waypoints ahead); a DESTINATION being moved rather than a corner; the ladder climbing while a commanded deviation is still settling, which fires the very rung the deviation was avoiding; and the operator's PROCEED covering anything but slow and hold - never the alarm, never a deviation, and never the helm (projectRoute / edgeAround in static/js/guard.js, the edge rung + guardTrack + guardOverrideOk in static/asv.html)." ;;
    amend_plan.py)     echo "  THE VESSEL HALF OF THE DEVIATION. A running plan can have its unflown remainder replaced without losing the run - which is the capability whose absence limited the clearance guard to the throttle, because upload_plan resets the index to zero and the only way to change a plan was to re-run it from waypoint one. Watch for: the index moving (it is upload_plan again); the flown prefix being discarded; _seg_start left on the old waypoint, which measures progress along a leg that no longer exists and skips a shortened amendment in the tick it arrives; an amendment failing to end a coast (a hull with the prop off cannot take a deviation); Engine.amend renaming the run, which would re-arm the end-of-plan chain; and any of its four gates going - arm, e-stop, running, not-holding, in that order so each is observable by the sentence it produces. A malformed route is a 409 in words, never a 500 (SimVcu.amend_plan / Engine.amend / /api/cmd/amend in asv_console.py)." ;;
    gate_endpoint.js)  echo "  THE LANE GATE'S ENDPOINT EXEMPTION HAS GONE WRONG AGAIN. gateLegClear is the law over the Rule 9 lane - no route leaves the pipeline without passing the same legClear the obstacle search obeyed - and it waives ONE thing: a block within 2*buf of the route's own start or goal, because a boat moored inside the buffer must still be led out and an arrival can end at a dock. The test used to ask only whether the BLOCK was near an endpoint, which says nothing about whether that endpoint is anywhere it should not be. Measured at New Castle over 475 Go-To routes: legPath shipped a fouling leg 0 times, and ONE shipped route passed 0.47 m from a charted pier with a 3 m buffer - narrowChannelLane moved it 3.45 -> 2.63 m, smoothTrack took it to 0.47 (it tests the moved VERTEX and never the legs either side of it), and this gate waved it through because the block sat 5.8 m from a goal that had 3.45 m of clear water. It asks whether the ENDPOINT ITSELF is blocked now. Watch for: the endpoint test being dropped (check 2); it being asked at 2*buf instead of the buffer, which calls a 4 m clearance a berth (2); the exemption going altogether, which re-routes a moored boat around its own dock (3, and buoy_lane 18); and the ASV-OWNS-THIS header going, which is what stops a re-vendor from asv_core silently deleting the fix (6). ⚠ The fixture matters: the exemption reads firstBlockAlong, so on a long head-on approach the first block is never near the goal and neither check can tell the fix from the fault - the world is a pile 2 m to starboard of a short final leg (gateLegClear in static/js/routing.js, which ASV OWNS)." ;;
    line_stats.js)     echo "  THE LINES CARD OR THE RUN-PROGRESS FIGURE HAS STOPPED DESCRIBING WHAT IT MEASURES. Both used to reset in ONE branch of onState - the TRANSITION into \"running\" - and every commanded motion goes through Engine._run_route, which sets run=\"running\" UNCONDITIONALLY. So a Go-To, an RTH, a transit or a routed re-approach commanded while something is already running never leaves the running state, and nothing reset: the per-line times stayed attached by POSITION to a line set the strike had changed (the old self-heal only fixed the array LENGTH, which a strike-one-add-one does not even alter), and runTotalM kept the PREVIOUS run's total so remaining exceeded it and the percentage clamped to 0 and stayed there. Each quantity is keyed on the thing it is a measure of now: the per-line times on the LINE SET, the progress fraction on the ROUTE (its length AND its waypoint count - two different routes can measure the same). Watch for: the key comparing lengths again; resetLineStats not stamping the key (which zeroes the stats on every tick after it); syncLineStats losing its call in accumLineTime; and routeRemainingM dropping `n`. ⚠ Check 11 is a WIRING check and a source grep cannot see reachability - an `if(false)` around the run-state reset survives it, recorded in the header (syncLineStats / lineSetKey / updateRunTime / routeRemainingM in static/asv.html). And review #23: the elapsed clock times the COMMANDED MOTION - onState must call startNewMotion() when S.run_seq moves (never on a resume, a re-approach, or a page that opened mid-run), accumRunTime must tick the motion's clock and the job's separately, and updateRunTime must write runTimeTip() into the row's title." ;;
    chart_ink.js)      echo "  READING THE CHART ITSELF has broken. NOAA's Maritime Chart Service DRAWS structures its own vector service does not carry - measured at New Castle, the nearest ENC object of ANY class to a drawn finger pier is 4.3 m away and that is the MAIN pier's outline; ENCDirect's identify at the same point returns seven AREAS and nothing else; and enc_berthing, the finest band S-57 defines, has no coverage there at all. So the scan subtracts everything the ENC explains from the chart's ink and sieves what is left. Watch for: the ASPECT gate going (a 4.9 x 1.9 m chart symbol passed a width-only gate on the live sweep and was reported as a structure); the PERPENDICULAR gate going, which is the ONE that separates a finger pier from a depth contour - they are drawn in the same grey at the same width and only the direction differs; detached marks being ENFORCED rather than reported (five at New Castle, every one foreshore symbology); the fold leaving rebuildNogo, where any control change silently discards it; a REFUSAL being cached as a clean read, because an unread tile is blank paper and reads exactly like open water; and the chart-read lines being drawn in the ENC's own red, which claims a warrant the console does not have. ⚠ The fixtures are the fragile part - five of these checks could not fail when written, all five because the mark under test was subtracted by the explained mask or the segment list had one entry (scanChart / classify in static/js/chartink.js, scanChartInk / ensureChartInk / rebuildNogo in static/asv.html)." ;;
    in_extremis.js)    echo " THE RUNG'S SELECTION TEST CHANGED ON 2026-09-19 AND THESE CHECKS ENCODE THE NEW CONTRACT: in extremis is a DANGER test, not a REACH test. The drift-only projection must reach within HELM_ENTRY_FRAC of the operator's buffer (half it) inside HELM_S (20 s, = HOLD_S - if stopping buys more than a decision margin, the HOLD rung is the right answer), and the banner must quote that projection rather than assert 'being set onto' a feature the code never measured the set against. Watch for: either constant widening back toward the old 45 s / whole-buffer test (check 5 and 5c); the canStop exemption being 'simplified' away, which would soften the rung for a boat with NO WAY ON, whose drift track IS its ground track and for whom a hold is what she is already doing (checks 6 and 6b - the Eastport loop); and 5c's fixture drifting so that its outside case reads clear for a sampling reason instead of the margin, which is how its first draft passed with the defect restored.  A SAFETY SUITE, and the one that decides whether the console may STEER. The ladder is clear -> slow -> hold -> helm, and the rung that matters is the LAST TWO: `hold` and `helm` are separated by a SECOND projection made with the engines notionally stopped, using drift alone. If that drift-only track is clear, taking the way off answers the situation and the console has no business steering; if it is not, stopping is the one thing that CERTAINLY fails, because the water is doing the carrying. That is measured, not argued - a hull lying stopped in a 2 kn stream makes 2.00 kn over the ground (tests/currents.py A and E), which is why the standing never-steers rule gives at this rung and only here. Watch for: the drift projection being dropped or short-circuited (helm becomes unreachable and the Eastport pier loop comes back); helm firing on any predicted entry (the console steering on every ordinary approach); the escape being scored through the WATER rather than with the set in it, which picks a heading that is clear through the water and downstream into the pier; the boxed-in case returning a least-bad heading instead of REFUSING, which is a confident-looking lie that still ends in the pier; the guard gate returning to run+not-holding, which is what excluded a station-keeping boat and was the reported bug; and the helm rung reverting to a Go-To instead of the dedicated /api/cmd/escape it commands now - a Go-To-shaped escape's arrival used to re-chain the end-of-plan RTH straight back into the hazard it had just steered clear of (tests/end_action.js 5b/16b, tests/escape_chain.py) (assess / escapeCourse / restoreVel in static/js/guard.js, clearanceGuard in static/asv.html). AND REVIEW #7 (10b-10h): from INSIDE the buffer escapeCourse walks each track - never within ESCAPE_HARD_M of the feature, out within the horizon, then a whole horizon clear, and the escape point at the end of THAT; clear headings tie-break on the WORST clearance along the track, then the clearance at its end, never ground distance; and there is NO fixed exit deadline (a 15 m buffer in a 4 kn set takes 11.7 s straight out, and a 10 s deadline read that as BOXED IN)." ;;
    coast.js)          echo "  COMING IN ON THE DRIFT - the release solve. The hull is one coast length Lc, so the distance to HALVE speed is Lc*ln2 from ANY release speed; a constant-deceleration ramp would scale it as v0^2, and check 3 is that discrimination - it is what stops the engine ramp quietly standing in for the physics. ⚠ THE HEADING IS THE ROUTE'S, NEVER THE COAST'S TO CHOOSE: an earlier cut picked the heading that best stemmed the set, which put the release point UP-SET of the berth and, at Eastport, 40 m inside the wharf (check 6). ⚠ AND THE MANOEUVRE ENDS ON A SPEED, NOT A POSITION - Lc is an estimate +/-1/3, which over a 50 m run is ~17 m of doubt against ~6 m of clear water at a tight berth, so no aim point is safe; ending on a speed means the error moves only WHERE power resumes, never how fast she arrives. The track is walked against the router's own keep-out model and walked PAST the berth by the certified clear water (checks 7, 8) (solveCoast / coastLc / coastRun in static/js/coast.js)." ;;
    coast_sim.py)      echo "  THE VESSEL HALF OF THE DRIFT-IN. Watch for: the decay collapsing back into the engine's 1.5 kn/s ramp (check 2 - the halving distance is the tell); THE COAST NEVER ENDING (check 3) - quadratic drag only asymptotes, so without the COAST_END_KN floor a boat that stops short of its berth glides for ever, never arriving, never holding, prop off, which was a real defect in the first cut and was found by DRIVING it, not reading it; a hull with no coast datum being given a default instead of None (check 4) - Lc needs mass, which two of the three shipped hulls do not carry in any form and the third carries only as prose, and the lateral leeway constants are not a substitute (they give the DriX a 1.6 m stopping distance); and a fresh plan inheriting a coast (check 6). ⚠ THIS SUITE MUST KEEP ITS sys.path.insert(0, APP): without it asv_console's `import currents` resolves to tests/currents.py, which runs ITS OWN suite and exits 0 - the symptom is this file printing another file's output and passing (COAST_LENGTH_M / COAST_END_KN / the speed block in SimVcu.tick)." ;;
    hold_point.js)     echo "  WHERE A BOAT IS ASKED TO HOLD, and how much water it has there. A hold point needs WORKING MARGIN, not merely to be outside the buffer - the water the SET moves the boat through while the ladder is deciding (holdMarginM, importing the ladder own HOLD_S), floored at hull scale. Under it the point MOVES, even when technically clear, and where the geometry allows a choice the berth is taken DOWN-SET of the hazard. Eastport 2026-09-03: HOME had 1.47 m of clear water, was not blocked at the BARE buffer, so holdTarget never consulted the search at all and the boat reached 1.74 m from it at 6.07 kn. ⚠ WATCH FOR A CHECK THAT DRIVES snapClearRadial DIRECTLY INSTEAD OF THROUGH holdTarget - the gate is what failed, and two mutations survived on exactly that (check 6g drives the real entry point; 6c2 stands in the band where the old and new rules disagree). Also the command-time half of the earlier Eastport work. A commanded route's end is a HOLD POINT, not merely a destination: a target inside the keep-out model is moved to the nearest clear water in ANY direction (a radial search - the existing one-axis nudge that splits a long leg finds the further water at a pier's END), with the whole hold DISC - buffer plus the radius the vessel is allowed to wander - clear, and the move is captured with the plan's reasoning and said on the banner. Only no clear water within the search cap remains a refusal, and it names the cap. Watch for: the radial search reverting to one axis; the snap margin dropping the hold radius (clear only at the centre, not the whole disc); a moved hold point going unreported; and the routed re-approach firing without authority or without the vessel actually asking (holdTarget / snapClearRadial / holdClearM in static/js/hold.js, planNogoRoute in static/js/passage.js, reapproachIfSetOff in static/asv.html)." ;;
    hold_station.py)   echo "  THE VESSEL HALF of the hold-point work: a station-keeping vessel does not drive a blind bearing back onto station from any range. Every holding command carries hold_clear_m, the radius certified clear round the hold point; inside it the direct re-approach is honest (a chord of a clear disc is clear), beyond it the vessel takes the way off and reports hold_wants_route, and Engine.reapproach() supplies a routed way back that KEEPS the run's behaviour rather than renaming it a Go-To. No hold_clear_m at all keeps the direct drive - the honest degrade. Watch for: the station-keep branch ignoring hold_clear_m; the direct band using the hold radius instead of the certified disc; reapproach() losing its holding gate; and hold_clear_m validation dropping (a bad value must be a 409 in words, never a 500) (the station-keep branch of SimVcu.tick, Engine.reapproach / Engine._hold_clear in asv_console.py). AND AN UPLOAD NEVER CHANGES WHAT THE BOAT IS DOING (7b-7e, 11c-11d): upload_plan must STAGE while the link is running and start() apply it, set_speed must carry into the staged plan, stop() must keep it, and Engine.upload must refuse under way - lose staging and a boat holding at a Go-To point drives off at transit speed the moment Upload is pressed (SimVcu.upload_plan / start / stop and Engine.upload in asv_console.py). AND REVIEW #8 (7f-7i, 11b, 11e-11g): SimVcu stop / estop / set_neutral call _end_hold(); start() keeps a resumed hold and restarts a finished plan at waypoint 0; tick() has an elif-moving branch with target_kn 0 so nothing makes way with no leg and no hold; Engine.reapproach passes continuing=True and _run_route refuses it, under the lock, unless run is running; Engine.start keeps the behavior on a resume. 11b's Stop must be a POST (api with {}) - as a GET it 404s and 11b passes or fails by timing." ;;
    escape_chain.py)   echo "  THE IN-EXTREMIS ESCAPE IS ITS OWN BEHAVIOUR, not a Go-To wearing one. Measured live at Eastport, 2026-09-03: the clearance guard steered clear of a pier via a Go-To, held there, and nine seconds later the end-of-plan Return-to-Home chain read that hold as \"the plan is over\" and sent the boat straight back toward the pier - a second escape fired three seconds after that, the safety intervention undoing itself on repeat. /api/cmd/escape marks the run behavior \"escape\", which tests/end_action.js (5b, 16b) proves rthPending() and the literal chain-fire condition in onState both refuse to ever chain from - this file proves the half those page checks cannot see, that the SERVER actually sets behavior to \"escape\" and not \"goto\" left over from Engine.escape() being built out of go_to(). Watch for: Engine.escape() reverting its behavior string; the escape losing its ARM gate; and a routed re-approach off an escape hold (Engine.reapproach, which reuses self.behavior) coming back as anything other than \"escape\" (Engine.escape in asv_console.py, the helm rung of clearanceGuard in static/asv.html)." ;;
    direct_turn.js)    echo "  THE DIRECT (racetrack) REVERSAL, or where it sits in the ladder, has moved. teardropTurn sweeps an arc of radius HALF THE LINE OFFSET whenever the hull can hold it - 15.75 m on a Z-Boat at 31.5 m spacing, against a hull radius of 2.06 m - and such an arc needs 15.75 m of clear water past the line end to bulge outboard. Where a wharf takes that water away the old ladder's only answer was the SAME arc swept inboard, back across 33 m of just-surveyed water, which is what Andy photographed on 2026-09-01 and called an inverted teardrop. The racetrack asks for the radius the boat actually has: two quarter-circles at minR joined by a straight, reaching minR past the line end AT ANY SPACING (check 3 is that invariant) and 32% shorter. Watch for: rung 1 changing - the gentle arc is what every unobstructed turn in every existing plan flies and check 8 holds it; the racetrack rung moving BELOW the inboard one, which puts the boat back over its own survey; the JOIN gate being loosened or unwired (checks 10/10a/10c, and turn_geometry 50-57): since 2026-09-19 turnJoinable refuses any shape that does not leave the line on hE and arrive on hF, which is every INBOARD semicircle - that rung was never the wharf fix, it returns the arc for the OPPOSITE transition and Andy's own plans shipped 10 of them. What answers 'turn AWAY from the dock' is the racetrack rung, and where even that will not fit the pair must go RED rather than ship a turn the boat cannot join; the arc step going back to a flat 3 m, which resolves a 3.2 m quarter-arc with ONE chord and rolls the vessel out 11 degrees off the next line (checks 1b/12); and the FLYABILITY gate being loosened or unwired (checks 10d/10e): since 2026-09-19 those are the only ladder-level checks that red when turnFlyable is neutered - 10b reaches its verdict through the join gate and stays green - and 10d is also the one check anywhere that catches the projection reading a flat 1 m approach radius instead of the hull's own. Its fixture borrows the 8 m profile on purpose: on a 1 m-approach hull the band of water that is outside the buffer from the DRAWN line and inside it from the FLOWN one is 0.38 m wide anywhere, which is no place to stand a fixture (racetrackTurn in static/js/core_turns.js, turnWithRetry and turnFlyable in static/js/turns.js)." ;;
    enc_roles.js)      echo "  An ENC ROLE lost its decided meaning. Every class the server requests carries its geometry in its name, and buildKeepouts' dispatch is an if/else chain on geometry where the FIRST match wins - so a role in the wrong branch does not error, it DROPS the feature silently. Check 8 is the general test (every requested form must arrive somewhere) and it exists because the FIRST version of it could not catch the bug it was written for: crossing 'bridge' into two branches still emits the ring, and a bucket count cannot see a feature that was never emitted. Watch for: bridge_span becoming a keep-out (it is the DECK - enforcing it refuses passage under every bridge on the chart, and New Castle NH alone charts 35 of them); a pylon/wreck/buoy POINT class moved into a ring-branch role; 'extra' - every remaining published layer, downloaded on entering an area - gaining any branch, which would make a harbour one solid keep-out; a cardinal buoy filed as chan_mark (it carries no CATLAM, so it would invent a Rule 9 fairway out of marks that describe a danger); and one class named under two roles, which fetches it twice and lets one copy be dropped while the other survives (ENC_ROLES in asv_console.py, buildKeepouts / nogoKind in static/js/keepouts.js)." ;;
    survey_order.js)   echo "  The RUNNING ORDER of a survey no longer matches what the manuals say (operations 9.9, technical 8.3, README). regionOrder (static/js/passage.js) must number runs across from the outermost line AWAY from the third click, start in the cell holding that line on its run as handed in, take the nearest cell next, sweep each cell from its lowest line entering every run at its end nearer the previous exit, and skip a line with no runs; commitPattern must APPEND (run ends, then turn:true joining points) and never sort; routePlan must route mission.waypoints in order and lane only leg 0; currentLegLine must match both ends within LINE_MATCH_M; and punchOut must keep the gate it quotes (antiParallel < 50, GAP_LINES = 4, turnMargin max(2, spacing/2)). If you changed the order on purpose, change the three documents with it." ;;
    turn_refusal.js)   echo "  ADD TO PLAN no longer refuses what it must, or refuses what it must not (Andy's call, 2026-09-16: a reversal every turn rung refused keeps the pattern OUT of the plan - that evening a 74-line plan had gone up twice with 17 of them as straight legs). punchOut must record each red join in patRed in the same statement as its patUnsafe pair (turn:true only for a refused REVERSAL, with why and by), rebuild patRed on every punch, and set patJoined only after its pair loop. punchRefusal must key on patClip - no punch refuses nothing, unless patDropped says the page threw the punch away - refuse an unfinished punch, count reversals only (a red HOP is Upload's to route again), and offer only remedies that can work: never the lead, the low TURN speed only for a track refusal or a teardrop plan, a wider spacing only for a teardrop plan, standoff for a navigation channel. updatePatReadout must gray out #sp_add with the reason as its title and fill #sp_refuse; commitPattern must ask AFTER flushing the pending re-punch and touch nothing when refused; applyWaterOffset must record patDropped and refresh the readout when it drops a punch - one tide update once turned a refused punch into 23 unclipped lines with no turns (punchRefusal / commitPattern / updatePatReadout / punchOut / applyWaterOffset in static/asv.html)." ;;
    strike_run.js)     echo "  STRIKING A PUNCHED RUN OFF broke, or the transits stopped rebuilding around the gap. The filter MUST sit after the ENC clip and before regionOrder: above the clip, one pattern line is several clipped runs and striking a short stub takes the long run on the far side with it (that is what got WorldView's build reverted); below the ordering, the serpentine stays numbered through a line that is not there. Strikes are MIDPOINTS, never indices - the runs are rebuilt on every change, and a midpoint stops naming anything when its run goes rather than naming the wrong one; it survives shortenSeg (symmetric lerp) and regionOrder (orders and re-orients, never splits), which is what makes the segment the operator clicks the run the filter matches. The rebuild is COALESCED and every reader flushes: punchOut refuses while one is in flight, so an un-serialised rebuild is DROPPED, and 400 ms is longer than it takes to press Delete then Add to plan - a commit inside that window commits the PRE-strike plan with every figure agreeing with itself. ⚠ Check 14/15: the reversal gate must admit a pair separated by a MISSING line (gapSpan, not a fixed 1.6 spacings) or a struck line yields a silent straight 180 no hull can track and no banner mentions. ⚠ Check 13 is a WIDTH RELATIONSHIP because the first halo was measurably invisible - 5 px under a 2.5 px stroke changed ONE pixel on the live canvas while every check passed (strikeSelectedRun / keptRuns / flushRepunch / punchNow in static/asv.html)." ;;
    end_action.js)     echo "  The cards no longer name where the run leaves the boat - an end-of-plan RTH hidden behind a 'loiter', or promised where the chain cannot fire (rthPending / endAction)." ;;
    rule9_scope.js)    echo "  WHERE COLREGS RULE 9 APPLIES has drifted. It applies ONLY within a narrow channel or fairway, and only on a TRANSIT. A narrow channel is a CHARTED OBJECT - S-57 FAIRWY (Fairway_area) and DRGARE (Dredged_Area), plus a buoyed lateral system - or water genuinely under NARROW_MAX_M wide. It is NOT a ray-march for walls within reach: that had no width test at all and laned 300 m of open bay while claiming compliance with a rule of the road. Watch for: the gate in narrowChannelLane being loosened; NARROW_MAX_M widened; a fairway becoming a KEEP-OUT (adding it to isArea in buildKeepouts looks tidy and refuses every route down a marked channel - test with area enforcement ON); routePlan laning routed detours again (a bare leg.length>1 lanes survey patterns); a pattern losing its {lane:false}; and lane:false skipping the smooth/gate/prune pipeline rather than just the offset - gateLegClear is a per-leg keep-out re-check and every route needs it. TSS is Rule 10 and recommended tracks are neither; keep them out (narrowChannelLane / channelPolys / routePlan in static/js, ENC_ROLES in asv_console.py)." ;;
    speed_modes.js)    echo "  Speed by ROLE broke. A survey run is three jobs - coverage, turns, transits - and each has its own selectable speed; the console GOVERNS which is live as the run moves between them. Watch for: the role being re-derived instead of read off runLineIdx/curTurn (a second classifier drifts from the one that bills the time, and then the speed the boat runs and the activity the recorder logs describe different moments); the governor writing mission.speeds (that is the OPERATOR'S setting - mission.speed beside it is what the boat is commanded NOW, two fields on purpose, the completion-field lesson one over); the clearance guard's override being ignored (safety outranks the role - while it holds the boat slow the governor stands off); commanding every frame rather than on change; commanding at all when the boat is not under autonomous command; and a heading ever being commanded - the console slows and NEVER steers. ⚠ The TURN radius is derived from the TURN speed (check 10): a hull holds v/omega, so a reversal must be built at the speed it is flown at (speedRole / roleSpeed / speedGovernor in static/asv.html, _norm_speeds in asv_console.py). AND THE RECONCILE (19a-19f): commandSpeed must remember the key, speedReconcile must compare it with the vessel's speed_key every frame and re-send after SPEED_RESEND_MS, and onState must call it right after the governor." ;;
    clearance_guard.js) echo "  A SAFETY SUITE. Two halves, both written after a DriX passed a wharf at 0.6 m with a 5 m buffer set (Pago Pago, 2026-08-28). (1) THE TURN LADDER: a refused reversal is retried on the OTHER SIDE and then at the slow radius before it is given up, and if every rung refuses the pair is flagged UNSAFE rather than shipped as a straight 180 the hull cannot track. That straight-leg fallback is what put the boat on the pier - refusing the turn removed the only geometry steering it away from the thing that refused it - so if check 12 is red, someone has restored it. (2) THE RUNTIME GUARD: clearanceM is the boat's live distance to the keep-out model, built from blocked()'s own primitives so the number on the card cannot disagree with the rule the route was cleared against; the guard alarms inside the buffer and commands the LOW SPEED when closing. It must never command a heading (check 16) and must never command anything unless the boat is under autonomous command (check 14) - the RC transmitter is master and is the true failsafe. Note check 10b: slowing only reshapes a TEARDROP, never a semicircle, whose radius is the line spacing (turnWithRetry / clearanceGuard / clearanceM). AND THE SLOW-DOWN IN LIEU OF A HOLD (15k-15o): slowLieu must be recorded when that rung fires, and while the level still reads hold the guard must re-ask whether slowing answers it from here and whether it was taken within SLOW_ANSWER_MS - lose either and a lost speed command leaves the boat standing on at full speed with no hold ever sent (clearanceGuard in static/asv.html). AND 15p: the slow rung's gate reads the speed_key the vessel reports, not roleSpeed(). AND 15q: the BOXED IN banner names the refusal - every heading enters, from outside the buffer; no heading gets out of it, from inside (c.m < buf)." ;;
    port_slew.js)      echo "  The port change stopped being a visible journey, or stopped arriving. The slew must LAND on the destination at the original zoom on EVERY exit - end of flight, operator's hand on the chart, a second port picked mid-flight - because a chart stranded between two bases is worse than the teleport this replaced. It must be driven by setTimeout on Date.now() elapsed and NEVER requestAnimationFrame (rAF is suspended in an occluded window; that is the same trap that froze the window-split mirror and the card-size observer). Watch for: BOAT-follow losing its !slew guard (a port change respawns the sim boat at the new base, so frame one already reports the destination and follow would make the flight a one-frame teleport); a fractional zoom (it goes in the tile KEY and URL - the tiles do not exist); a lost antimeridian unwrap (Nome to Kamchatka flies the long way, across the Atlantic); and resetForNewArea no longer refetching the keep-out model, which is the defect this suite was written with - clearing nogo.ready makes BOTH arms of the re-extract in onState unreachable, so the console sits at the new base planning DIRECT in a sea it has never read (planSlew / slewAt / endSlew / resetForNewArea in static/asv.html)." ;;
    off_track.js)      echo "  The Intent card's 'off track' is measuring the wrong thing again. It is the SIGNED PERPENDICULAR from the leg being FLOWN (rr[idx-1] -> rr[idx]) and nothing else - never the range to the NEAREST survey line, which is what it used to be and which described a line the boat was not following on every transit, turn, Go-To and RTH. Watch for: a clamped t (point-to-segment is range-to-waypoint wearing the off-track label), a Math.abs or Math.hypot (the card's 'left' branch becomes unreachable, as it was for the whole of this console's life), or a nearest-line DISTANCE re-added to updateActiveLine - that function resolves an INDEX for the chart highlight and must publish no distance (offTrack / updateActiveLine in static/asv.html)." ;;
    turn_geometry.js)  echo "  A survey reversal no longer ends on the next line aligned, or asks the boat to hold a radius tighter than it can (teardropTurn in static/asv.html)." ;;
    roc_tracks.py)     echo "  A recovery point no longer lands where the offset says, the staged/active HOME gate leaks, a vessel-derived default stopped tracking the vessel, or one malformed NMEA sentence is dropping the whole GPS link (roc_tracks.py)." ;;
    ais_range.py)      echo "  The display range no longer filters the collected area correctly - filtering by box rather than range circle, filtering a LAKE, or exceeding what was collected (/api/ais + /api/ais/radius)." ;;
    mission_store.py)  echo "  THE PLAN FILE LOST A PROTECTION. Watch for: load_mission answering a locked or corrupt file with an EMPTY plan (only FileNotFoundError is no plan); reads leaving the writer's lock; the replace no longer retried on PermissionError (WinError 5); set_speed writing the plan file again; Engine._run_route reading the file or its speed instead of mission_params and the link's speed_key; and _keep_previous_plan not keeping, or keeping on settings-only saves (_read_mission_file / save_mission / _keep_previous_plan / _replace_retrying in asv_console.py). AND 11-14 (review #10): check_plan_body refuses a body that is not a plan before anything is written; save_mission compares and bumps rev under _mission_lock and _stored_rev refuses a locked file; load_mission carries rev; do_POST wraps _dispatch_post so every route answers." ;;
    live_speed.py)     echo "  A speed change is not reaching the vessel - speed over ground no longer follows the commanded speed (SimVcu.set_speed / Engine.set_speed / /api/cmd/speed). AND it must not write mission.json: the commanded speed is the vessel's, and the next Go-To takes it from the link (Engine._run_route)." ;;
    estop_chain.py)    echo "  THE SAFETY CHAIN. E-STOP is no longer reaching the vessel, no longer latching, or no longer letting go cleanly (Engine.set_estop / SimVcu.estop / the _require guards / /api/cmd/estop). Check 5b is the seam itself: the console's own flags can all look right while the boat is never told. Checks 15-15b (review #27): a latch the link refuses must still latch the console (set_estop commands the link FIRST and latches whatever it answers), and a refused release must leave it latched." ;;
    run_link_control.py) echo "  Run/link control broke: pause no longer holds the boat (or holds it but loses the leg - pause is NOT stop), transit skips its route sanitizing or arm gate, reset stopped being a clean sim power-cycle (or stopped REFUSING on a real link), or disconnect leaves a zombie link that still takes commands (Engine.pause/transit/reset/connect/disconnect in asv_console.py). AND 15-15f, THE FRAME RACE: Engine._run notes _cmd_gen before link.tick() and drops a frame if it moved; stop / pause / start / set_estop / set_armed(off) / _run_route call _commanded() under the lock. Those checks are IN-PROCESS - a SimVcu subclass fires the command from inside tick(), the only way to put it in that window on demand. AND 3c / 16 (review #9): _sanitize_route refuses a route over ROUTE_MAX_WPTS WHOLE via too_many_wpts, never slices it; Engine.upload applies the same bound to the saved plan; state publishes route_max_wpts; and the limit must stay at or above 6435, the largest plan in the session logs. AND 17-17g (review #12): Engine._run must restart _run_loop after _loop_faulted, which records loop_fault {error, count, since}, prints each raising LINE once per episode, and pushes the state in its own try - and both reports (_loop_faulted's and _loop_clean's print) sit in a try, because a write to a console window that has gone raises and would end the loop after all; _loop_clean takes the fault down only after LOOP_FAULT_CLEAR_TICKS clean ticks in a row; connect() clears it; state publishes loop_fault and streaming (_link is not None)." ;;
    home_spawn.py)     echo "  Home/spawn broke. sethome has TWO sources and each lands on its own check: an explicit lat/lon must be honoured exactly (2a) and NO position must still capture the boat's LIVE fix (2b) - never a dead link's stale status (Engine.set_home's guards + connect()'s status clear). Half a coordinate must refuse rather than fall back (2c), and a malformed one must be a refusal that NAMES the rule (2d) - a bare float() would be turned into a generic 500 by _dispatch_post's catch-all, which is the handler falling over, not refusing. NOTE 2a asserts the OPPOSITE of what this suite said before 2026-08-08, when a supplied position was discarded; that reversal was the operator's explicit call - read the docstring before 'restoring' it. If this suite ABORTS at 2e rather than failing a check, the console STOPPED ANSWERING partway through - a malformed home reached the engine, which is what the range guard in Engine.set_home exists to prevent. Also: RTH no longer closes on home, or spawn stopped validating / stopped being a full power-cycle AT the point (the /api/cmd/spawn handler + Engine.reset)." ;;
    energy_chartinfo.py) echo "  Energy override or chart info broke: the override no longer snaps/holds the tank (SimVcu.set_unlimited_energy + the tick's _unlimited_energy branch), the engine's state() force-fill is gone (the only layer when DISCONNECTED), or /api/chartinfo stopped serving its exact-key cache / answering a malformed bbox with the 400 usage line (fetch_chart_info / _serve_chartinfo)." ;;
    enc_extract.py)    echo "  The ENC feature extract broke: a malformed bbox is no longer a 400 with the ENC usage, the cache stopped answering (or started REWRITING itself on read), the per-request shallow retag is gone/baked-in/off-by-one at the boundary, or a SHARED helper regressed - _bbox_key and _bbox_from_query serve BOTH /api/enc and /api/chartinfo, so check the twin too (fetch_enc_features / _serve_enc in asv_console.py)." ;;
    env_water.py)      echo "  Env/water broke: a manual env override no longer REACHES the running boat (EnvMonitor.field / env_set_kn), disabling no longer returns it to calm (every suite that assumes calm water inherits that), the field-merge/clear/auto semantics regressed, or a non-numeric value is a dropped connection again instead of a 400 - BOTH branches sit before the dispatch try, so their guards are local (the /api/env + /api/waterlevel handlers in _dispatch_post)." ;;
    log_routes.py)     echo "  The log routes broke: a client logevent with a data key named kind/self drops the connection again (the rename in the /api/logevent handler - that branch has NO dispatch-try net), the record shape stopped being FLAT (playback reads it), /api/logs lost the live session, or safe_log_path serves something that is not a bare asv_*.jsonl inside LOG_DIR. NOTE this suite runs the console WITH logging on - every other harness passes --no-log, which masks the logevent branch entirely." ;;
    data_routes.py)    echo "  A data route broke: the vessel switch lost its SAFE gate (409 while armed) or stopped respawning (the energy gauge no longer flips), the comms password LEAKS (echoed by the GET, written to comms_config.json, or unredacted in the session log - three separate paths), the mode whitelist stores garbage, /api/tide stopped answering, or the ROC HTTP face lost an error mapping (unknown op 400 / bad request 400 / feed-unknown 404). Runs WITH logging on for the redaction check." ;;
    ais_error_frames.py) echo "  The upstream error frame is being swallowed again: an {error:...} text frame from aisstream must surface as the source note (state error), survive the quiet-box timeout re-stamp, and clear when real data flows (_frame_error + the read loop in ais_service.py - CRLF file). Before the fix a dead key read as connected, zero reports, forever." ;;
    completion_modes.py) echo "  The operator's END-OF-PLAN SETTING and the completion of the run IN PROGRESS have been conflated again - a behaviour is writing the setting, or a plan run is ignoring it (plan_completion / Engine.run_completion)." ;;
    docs_valid.py)     echo "  A generated document is not a package a reader will open, or a code block did not reach the page. Rebuild with: cd tools && node build_docs.js  - a helper that returns a LIST must be flattened (write() in docx_kit.js)." ;;
    tide_note.py)      echo "  The tide card is repeating one upstream failure as two sentences again, or has stopped naming which series were lost (tide_note in asv_console.py)." ;;
    http_contract.py)  echo "  A POST handler is breaking _dispatch_post's (code, obj) contract, a GET handler has a path that commits NO response, or something raised while serving. A handler can answer the client CORRECTLY and still take down its thread, so the check on the server's own log is the one no client check can see." ;;
    build_id.py)       echo "  The console no longer knows its own version (review #15): BuildWatch must fingerprint BUILD_PY + static/*.html + static/js/*.js by CONTENT (CRLF normalized, names relative to the program folder), re-read only on a size/mtime change at most every BUILD_RECHECK_S; state publishes build (BUILD.boot) and build_on_disk (BUILD.current()); do_GET '/' replaces PAGE_BUILD_TOKEN with BUILD.current(force=True)." ;;
    build_check.js)    echo "  The page no longer says when it and the console are different versions (review #15): buildCheck, called from onFrame after consoleHealth, shows #buildPill RESTART CONSOLE when build != build_on_disk or the state has no build, RELOAD PAGE when a filled-in (12-hex) PAGE_BUILD != build, and otherwise hides it - display none, empty text and title." ;;
    frame_health.js)   echo "  The page can fail without a sign again (review #12). onFrame must catch every onState error and count it (FRAME_FAULT_AFTER = 3 says CONSOLE FAULT, logged once as page_frame_fault; FRAME_CLEAR_AFTER = 8 clean frames IN A ROW take it down); consoleHealth runs on every frame AND from setInterval in connect(), says TELEMETRY STALE (dot lost, pill stale) after STALE_AFTER_MS with S.streaming, says S.loop_fault after the page fault, and hides only a banner matching HEALTH_BANNER. And review #29: stallWatch must run on setInterval(STALL_TICK_MS), write page_stall with stallContext() when a gap longer than STALL_MS BEGAN on screen (stallOnScreen, taken at the tick before the gap) wherever it ended - never for a gap that began hidden, which is the browser's throttling - and say a stall over STALL_SAY_MS once per STALL_SAY_EVERY_MS, and only on a page that is on screen." ;;
    plan_save.js)      echo "  The page is saving the plan blind again: flushMission must send missionRev, adopt the rev the console answers, send one save at a time, stop and say PLAN NOT SAVED on a 409, and say any other failure; loadMission must take rev. AND 7-11 (review #11): flushMission refuses until loadMission has loaded a plan (missionLoaded), a failed load retries every MISSION_LOAD_RETRY_MS, and resetMission / clearPlan / dropHeldSurvey pass {always: true} to guiConfirm." ;;
